    "turn_text": "really is extraordinary and I had a meeting some years ago this before the pandemic with a group of community college students from my district And they were talking to me about, and it came up in a kind of an off-handed way about the food banks they had on campus for students. And I was astonished that each of them, and they were going to three different community colleges, all had food banks on campus. This was still before the pandemic. So the economy was strong. far stronger than during the recession, fully recovered. And yet the hunger was greater than ever. And it really pointed to me of some strong structural problems in the economy, that even when it was doing well, it wasn't doing well for millions and millions of people to the point that college kids, not only at Munich College, but the state colleges of the private universities were going hungry. introduce a bill to try to expand the free and reduced lunch program that we have in K-12 up through community college. And that was the genesis of the Food for Thought Act. And we've been working to get that passed ever since and trying to address broader issues of hunger and homelessness as well.",
